Notes on the runs
  run 1: all 20 applies ran in under a second (script bug, fixed); no probes
  run 2: applies spread over the run; no probes after the applies
  run 3: committed script
  run 4: committed script
  run 5: committed script
  run 6: committed script and tool
  run 7: committed script and tool
  run 8: committed script and tool
  run 9: committed tree 98bdda2, nothing uncommitted
  run 10: tree ea04f9e plus the TRACE_MEMORY setting of load-test.sh (not yet committed), trace-memory off
  run 11: tree ea04f9e plus the TRACE_MEMORY setting of load-test.sh (not yet committed), trace-memory 8MB
  run 12: tree ea04f9e plus the TRACE_MEMORY setting of load-test.sh (not yet committed), trace-memory 16MB
  run 13: tree ea04f9e plus the TRACE_MEMORY setting of load-test.sh (not yet committed), trace-memory 32MB, the old default
  run 14: tree fff3e3b plus uncommitted edits to the README, the changelog and the results files (no code), the final code, trace-memory at its new default (8 MB)
  Runs 10 to 14 ran on a quiet machine (nothing else was building or testing); their 'others' column leaves out this session's own
  processes, as the earlier runs do. Runs 10 to 13 measure memory: results/memory-test.log. Run 14's full output is below.
  'records' compares the trace log with what BareProxy was sent (HTTP requests, WebSocket handshakes and the probes): equal in every run.
  Connections: HTTP/1.1 used 16 and WebSocket 20 in every run, none reset and none closed by the server. HTTP/2 opened 4 to 10 in the
  runs. The tool records when the last connection of each kind was opened (the 'last open' column in run 9): 0.0 s for all three kinds,
  so every connection was opened at the start and none was opened or replaced during the 20 applies. The extra HTTP/2 connections are
  most likely the first requests of each client dialling in parallel before its first connection is up; that was not checked further.

